l":"Human Mutation","paperid":"9961341","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Human Mutation","volume":"2023"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85176617210"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-bdce5fea-ce33-41ca-a038-a9b164e3ded1"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"A likelihood ratio approach for utilizing case-control data in the clinical classification of rare sequence variants : Application to BRCA1 and BRCA2","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-714586","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"It is often said that election processes depend on trust for their legitimacy. Trust is therefore crucial to the sustainability of democracy. It also seems clear that patterns and forms of trust in Western democracies are changing in tandem with digitalization. This paper explores some possible inflections of the concept of trust in the ‘age of digitalization’ by examining its articulation by computer scientists doing research on digital voting and by contrasting these articulations with a Science and Technology Studies (STS) analysis of trust in the Danish voting process. The motivation for characterizing and discussing these views of trust, is that it matters deeply how trust is conceptualized in relation to voting, democracy and beyond, and that today computer science as an engineering, scientific and intellectual field increasingly affects how we perceive of trust – indeed, how we organize social life and come to think about the world we live in. If Digitalization appears to be implicated in the current transformation of trust, then the questions of how trust is defined, by whom, and with what implications are open and acute matters in need of more general awareness and careful attention.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Gad, Christopher"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776368"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Democracy","Digital technology","Elections","Trust","Voting"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"trusting elections complexities and risks of digital voting in denmark","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17530350.2023.2246991","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was supported by The Danish Council for Strategic Research [grant number 10-092309]."],"id":"S01-000.00-776368","identifier":[{"id":"85170662605","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-0ab3de8e-0fe5-4542-bb42-b9a9ca947c5e"],"keyword":["Democracy","Digital Technology","Elections","Trust","Voting"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1E5BAB96AA9F060DE8C53875D18BBADE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/108495256/Trusting_elections_complexities_and_risks_of_digital_voting_in_Denmark.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101605623/2ndResubmission_Trust_in_Elections_Final.docx","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17530350.2023.2246991","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"gadpau","last":"Gad","locid":"d55dab65-fffe-4da4-a383-cfcf5ee3a8cd","name":"Gad, Christopher","orcid":"0000-0002-1631-5483","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/17530350.2023.2246991","issn":["17530350","17530369"],"issn_org":["17530350"],"journal":"Journal of Cultural Economy","pages":"1-18","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"Journal of Cultural Economy"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85170662605"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-0ab3de8e-0fe5-4542-bb42-b9a9ca947c5e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Trusting elections : complexities and risks of digital voting in Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776368","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"author_count":1,"contributors":["Gad, Christopher"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-385020"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"competing versions of trust in the making of democracy","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-385020","ids":["itu-fff3f73b-15da-4639-8593-d5ef29f9c0ec"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-AE764F92908170E837BBE5E783AF8868"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"gadpau","last":"Gad","locid":"d55dab65-fffe-4da4-a383-cfcf5ee3a8cd","name":"Gad, Christopher","orcid":"0000-0002-1631-5483","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fff3f73b-15da-4639-8593-d5ef29f9c0ec"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Competing Versions of Trust in the Making of Democracy","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-385020","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"This paper explores how the Danish school sector currently performs and reworks students’ engagement with digitalization through prototyping. Public schooling is routinely mobilized by the state as part of the solution to perceived societal problems. By law, Danish schooling should prepare students for participation, co-responsibility, rights, and duties in a democratic society. Recently, this includes preparing students for life in an increasingly digitalized democracy. We investigate the Ministry of Children and Education’s trial of ‘technology comprehension’ (2018-2021). This was an experimental effort to determine how to introduce ‘understanding of technology’ into compulsory schooling as a generally formative, creativeconstructive, and critical subject matter combining societal reflection, computer science and design approaches. The curriculum suggested that design approaches, especially, were conducive to agency and empowerment in digital democratic life, making it imperative that students learn to materialize digital artifacts through prototyping. This takes prototyping beyond its traditional use in design and systems development, making experimentalism central to the conduct of citizenship and social life. We examine prototyping across the trial: the curriculum; the trial’s design; the classroom; and the trial’s evaluation. We argue that prototyping functions as a device for intervention in the complexity and uncertainty of a digital democratic future. In this situation, the capacity of prototyping is to keep matters of concern both open and closed across scales, and bind different sites of the trial together. We critically examine the role of the prototype in a democracy in ’perpetual beta’ and the response: educating students to cultivate a design attitude.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","Gad, Christopher"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776437"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Design based learning","Digital literacy","Prototyping","Technology comprehension"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"prototyping the future prototyping citizens the trial of technology comprehension in danish schooling","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-776437","ids":["itu-abe568dd-605c-419f-8b00-3fbdfa39be6e"],"keyword":["Democratic citizenship in education","Design-based learning","Digital literacy","Prototyping","Technology comprehension"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E2CDC10E25C8F98888043029AAAA139B"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simy Kaur","key":"gahooniapau","last":"Gahoonia","locid":"6c543b1d-8137-489d-b444-d17921fc9de9","name":"Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","orcid":"0000-0001-9668-6868","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"gadpau","last":"Gad","locid":"d55dab65-fffe-4da4-a383-cfcf5ee3a8cd","name":"Gad, Christopher","orcid":"0000-0002-1631-5483","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-abe568dd-605c-419f-8b00-3fbdfa39be6e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Prototyping the future, prototyping citizens: The trial of Technology Comprehension in Danish Schooling","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776437","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Keywords: Socio-medical diagnosis, research through design and art, encountering the state, This talk is a report from an ongoing research trough art/design project on giving expression to experiences of parents to children undergoing socio-medical diagnosis (udredning) including experiences of encountering ‘the state’ in this situation. The project’s current aim is transforming interviews with such parents into an interactive audio-visual art installation intended to provoke reflections and a broader conversation on the matter. Today, Danish citizens has arguably become increasingly responsible for managing their various affairs in relation to the state. This is due to many causes, including digitalization. Such responsibilities arguably intensify in already emotionally challenging situations, such as e.g. undergoing divorce or being unemployed. Parents to children undergoing diagnosis express that they feel like becoming ‘project managers’ or ‘caseworkers’ for their children’s affairs and the lives of their families. The institutions with which they must engage quickly multiply and the state seems to be experienced as unexpectedly complex - more like a ’semiotic haze’ (Fabian Muniesa) rather than like a coordinated ‘whole’. Among other things, such parents become the main choreographers of an increasingly complex story of their child, which is important not only in their various encounters with institutions and for the trajectory of diagnosis and living with such, but also in managing relations to their families, neighbours, and friends. This talk will present how this research and art/design project developed from initial ideas to the resulting art-installation, which it is the plan to make public in the fall of 2022.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Gad, Christopher","Fritsch, Jonas","Jacobsen, Mogens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383610"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Interactive audio visual art","Parent experiences","Research through Design","Socio medical diagnosis","State encounters"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"expressing undergoing diagnosis","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383610","ids":["itu-a0c1cec3-631b-4fc3-9d53-d562c8dfe0f6"],"keyword":["Interactive audio-visual art","Parent experiences","Research through design","Socio-medical diagnosis","State encounters"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-7588C96A7709CD64798E33CE29A04302"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"gadpau","last":"Gad","locid":"d55dab65-fffe-4da4-a383-cfcf5ee3a8cd","name":"Gad, Christopher","orcid":"0000-0002-1631-5483","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas","key":"fritschpau","last":"Fritsch","locid":"d37cba38-dff2-4c27-a515-226cfadfd849","name":"Fritsch, Jonas","orcid":"0000-0001-9065-2843","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mogens","key":"jacobsenpau","last":"Jacobsen","name":"Jacobsen, Mogens","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a0c1cec3-631b-4fc3-9d53-d562c8dfe0f6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Expressing undergoing Diagnosis","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383610","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Objectives Physical inactivity and sedentary behaviour are associated with higher breast cancer risk in observational studies, but ascribing causality is difficult. Mendelian randomisation (MR) assesses causality by simulating randomised trial groups using genotype. We assessed whether lifelong physical activity or sedentary time, assessed using genotype, may be causally associated with breast cancer risk overall, pre/post-menopause, and by case-groups defined by tumour characteristics. Methods We performed two-sample inverse-variance-weighted MR using individual-level Breast Cancer Association Consortium case-control data from 130 957 European-ancestry women (69 838 invasive cases), and published UK Biobank data (n=91 105-377 234). Genetic instruments were single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) associated in UK Biobank with wrist-worn accelerometer-measured overall physical activity (n snps =5) or sedentary time (n snps =6), or accelerometer-measured (n snps =1) or self-reported (n snps =5) vigorous physical activity. Results Greater genetically-predicted overall activity was associated with lower breast cancer overall risk (OR=0.59; 95% confidence interval (CI) 0.42 to 0.83 per-standard deviation (SD;∼8 milligravities acceleration)) and for most case-groups. Genetically-predicted vigorous activity was associated with lower risk of pre/perimenopausal breast cancer (OR=0.62; 95% CI 0.45 to 0.87,≥3 vs. 0 self-reported days/week), with consistent estimates for most case-groups. Greater genetically-predicted sedentary time was associated with higher hormone-receptor-negative tumour risk (OR=1.77; 95% CI 1.07 to 2.92 per-SD (∼7% time spent sedentary)), with elevated estimates for most case-groups. Results were robust to sensitivity analyses examining pleiotropy (including weighted-median-MR, MR-Egger). Conclusion Our study provides strong evidence that greater overall physical activity, greater vigorous activity, and lower sedentary time are likely to reduce breast cancer risk. More widespread adoption of active lifestyles may reduce the burden from the most common cancer in women.","author_count":158,"contributors":["Dixon-Suen, Suzanne C.","Lewis, Sarah J.","Martin, Richard M.","English, Dallas R.","Boyle, Terry","Giles, Graham G.","Michailidou, Kyriaki","Bolla, Manjeet K.","Wang, Qin","Dennis, Joe","Lush, Michael","Investigators, Abctb","Ahearn, Thomas U.","Ambrosone, Christine B.","Andrulis, Irene L.","Anton-Culver, Hoda","Arndt, Volker","Aronson, Kristan J.","Augustinsson, Annelie","Auvinen, Päivi","Freeman, Laura E.Beane","Becher, Heiko","Beckmann, Matthias W.","Behrens, Sabine","Bermisheva, Marina","Blomqvist, Carl","Bogdanova, Natalia V.","Bojesen, Stig E","Bonanni, Bernardo","Brenner, Hermann","Brüning, Thomas","Buys, Saundra S.","Camp, Nicola J.","Campa, Daniele","Canzian, Federico","Castelao, Jose E.","Cessna, Melissa H.","Chang-Claude, Jenny","Chanock, Stephen J.","Clarke, Christine L.","Conroy, Don M.","Couch, Fergus J.","Cox, Angela","Cross, Simon S.","Czene, Kamila","Daly, Mary B.","Devilee, Peter","Dörk, Thilo","Dwek, Miriam","Eccles, Diana M.","Eliassen, A. Heather","Engel, Christoph","Eriksson, Mikael","Evans, D. Gareth","Fasching, Peter A.","Fletcher, Olivia","Flyger, Henrik","Fritschi, Lin","Gabrielson, Marike","Gago-Dominguez, Manuela","García-Closas, Montserrat","García-Sáenz, José A.","Goldberg, Mark S.","Guénel, Pascal","Gündert, Melanie","Hahnen, Eric","Haiman, Christopher A.","Häberle, Lothar","Håkansson, Niclas","Hall, Per","Hamann, Ute","Hart, Steven N.","Harvie, Michelle","Hillemanns, Peter","Hollestelle, Antoinette","Hooning, Maartje J.","Hoppe, Reiner","Hopper, John","Howell, Anthony","Hunter, David J.","Jakubowska, Anna","Janni, Wolfgang","John, Esther M.","Jung, Audrey","Kaaks, Rudolf","Keeman, Renske","Kitahara, Cari M.","Koutros, Stella","Kraft, Peter","Kristensen, Vessela N.","Kubelka-Sabit, Katerina","Kurian, Allison W.","Lacey, James V.","Lambrechts, Diether","Le Marchand, Loic","Lindblom, Annika","Loibl, Sibylle","Lubiński, Jan","Mannermaa, Arto","Manoochehri, Mehdi","Margolin, Sara","Martinez, Maria Elena","Mavroudis, Dimitrios","Menon, Usha","Mulligan, Anna Marie","Murphy, Rachel A.","Collaborators, Nbcs","Nevanlinna, Heli","Nevelsteen, Ines","Newman, William G.","Offit, Kenneth","Olshan, Andrew F.","Olsson, Håkan","Orr, Nick","Patel, Alpa","Peto, Julian","Plaseska-Karanfilska, Dijana","Presneau, Nadege","Rack, Brigitte","Radice, Paolo","Rees-Punia, Erika","Rennert, Gad","Rennert, Hedy S.","Romero, Atocha","Saloustros, Emmanouil","Sandler, Dale P.","Schmidt, Marjanka K.","Schmutzler, Rita K.","Schwentner, Lukas","Scott, Christopher","Shah, Mitul","Shu, Xiao Ou","Simard, Jacques","Southey, Melissa C.","Stone, Jennifer","Surowy, Harald","Swerdlow, Anthony J.","Tamimi, Rulla M.","Tapper, William J.","Taylor, Jack A.","Terry, Mary Beth","Tollenaar, Rob A.E.M.","Troester, Melissa A.","Truong, Thérèse","Untch, Michael","Vachon, Celine M.","Joseph, Vijai","Wappenschmidt, Barbara","Weinberg, Clarice R.","Wolk, Alicja","Yannoukakos, Drakoulis","Zheng, Wei","Ziogas, Argyrios","Dunning, Alison M.","Pharoah, Paul D.P.","Easton, Douglas F.","Milne, Roger L.","Lynch, Brigid M."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-507772"],"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Belgium","Canada","Cyprus","Finland","France","Germany","Greece","Israel","Italy","Netherlands","North Macedonia","Norway","Poland","Russia","Spain","Sweden","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","American Cancer Society","Antoni van Leeuwenhoek Hospital","Baker IDI Heart & Diabetes Institute","British Columbia Cancer Agency","Cancer Council Victoria","City of Hope National Med Center","Clalit Health Services","Clinical Hospital Acibadem Sistina","Columbia University","Cornell Univers