{"abstract":"According to many visions for smart grids, consumers will come to play a more ‘active’ role in the energy systems of tomorrow. In this paper, we examine how the future ‘flexible electricity consumer’ is imagined in the Danish National Smart Grid Strategy. Our analysis of reports produced by the national Smart Grid Network shows that this vision relies on a techno-centric and rather ‘inflexible’ consumer figuration. However, rather than adopting a conventional social science approach in order to criticize this narrow imaginary, we show that potentials for critique and alternatives can be found internally in the Smart Grid Network. Paying attention to different stories, we thus aim to characterize particular forms of ‘infra-critique’ and ‘infra-reflexivity’ emerging from within the field. This mode of reflexivity, we argue, opens up to more flexible and reflexive conceptions of the ‘flexible electricity consumer’ as well as more flexible relations between ‘the technical’ and ‘the social.’","author_count":2,"contributors":["Schick, Lea","Gad, Christopher"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383593"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Smart grid Energy Consumer User figuration STS Infra reflexivity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"flexible and inflexible energy engagements a study of the danish smart grid strategy","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2214629615300360","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383593","identifier":[{"id":"84945437228","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-9fd5757f-69d3-402f-b35f-6f36e1077cd2"],"keyword":["smart grid, energy, consumer, user figuration, STS, infra-reflexivity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-393A4371EF146DA1F918500CB26DC8E4"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lea","key":"schickpau","last":"Schick","name":"Schick, Lea","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"gadpau","last":"Gad","locid":"d55dab65-fffe-4da4-a383-cfcf5ee3a8cd","name":"Gad, Christopher","orcid":"0000-0002-1631-5483","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.erss.2015.08.013","issn":["22146296"],"journal":"Energy Research and Social Science","pages":"51-59","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Energy Research and Social Science","title_org":"Energy Research & Social Science","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84945437228"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9fd5757f-69d3-402f-b35f-6f36e1077cd2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Flexible and Inflexible Energy Engagements – a Study of the Danish Smart Grid Strategy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383593","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Proponents of computational thinking use the concept to account for what they perceive as important generalizable aspects of human thought (Wing 2011, National Research Council USA 2010, 2011). Simultaneously, the concept is employed to designate an ambitious pedagogical programme, in which computational thinking can be taught as a skill for the digitally literate 21st century (ibid.). As such, CT is seen both as an innate human capacity and a programme for developing future oriented skills - both for individuals and for populations at large. This paper explores what we perceive as conceptual slippage within the computational thinking concept, as it moves between the descriptive and promotional modes described above. We consider the implications of this slippage through various conceptual apparatuses available within STS – since these approaches are already critical of distinctions between the innate vs the learned, the normative and the descriptive. This exercise sets out the ground for considering CT – its promises and perils - as a growing arena for research in which the analytic techniques of STS are both needed and challenged.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Gad, Christopher","Douglas-Jones, Rachel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384525"],"event":{"end":"2015-05-29","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2015-05-27","title":"2nd Nordic Science and Technology Studies (STS) Conference","year":"2015"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computational thinking","Conceptual Slippage","Digital literacy","Pedagogy","Science and Technology Studies STS"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"promises and perils of computational thinking a discussion piece","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384525","ids":["itu-ddc571f9-c881-4bba-b01e-ec330f52979d"],"keyword":["Computational Thinking","Conceptual Slippage","Digital Literacy","Pedagogy","Science and Technology Studies (STS)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FFD542C076E22E3A57F151C299E3A061"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"gadpau","last":"Gad","locid":"d55dab65-fffe-4da4-a383-cfcf5ee3a8cd","name":"Gad, Christopher","orcid":"0000-0002-1631-5483","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ddc571f9-c881-4bba-b01e-ec330f52979d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Promises and perils of computational thinking : A discussion piece","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384525","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma (PTC) is the most common type of thyroid cancer. Here, we describe the genomic landscape of 496 PTCs. We observed a low frequency of somatic alterations (relative to other carcinomas) and extended the set of known PTC driver alterations to include EIF1AX, PPM1D, and CHEK2 and diverse gene fusions. These discoveries reduced the fraction of PTC cases with unknown oncogenic driver from 25% to 3.5%. Combined analyses of genomic variants, gene expression, and methylation demonstrated that different driver groups lead to different pathologies with distinct signaling and differentiation characteristics. Similarly, we identified distinct molecular subgroups of BRAF-mutant tumors, and multidimensional analyses highlighted a potential involvement of oncomiRs in less-differentiated subgroups. Our results propose a reclassification of thyroid cancers into molecular subtypes that better reflect their underlying signaling and differentiation properties, which has the potential to improve their pathological classification and better inform the management of the disease.","author_count":243,"contributors":["Agrawal, Nishant","Akbani, Rehan","Aksoy, B. Arman","Ally, Adrian","Arachchi, Harindra","Asa, Sylvia L.","Auman, J. Todd","Balasundaram, Miruna","Balu, Saianand","Baylin, Stephen B.","Behera, Madhusmita","Bernard, Brady","Beroukhim, Rameen","Bishop, Justin A.","Black, Aaron D.","Bodenheimer, Tom","Boice, Lori","Bootwalla, Moiz S.","Bowen, Jay","Bowlby, Reanne","Bristow, Christopher A.","Brookens, Robin","Brooks, Denise","Bryant, Robert","Buda, Elizabeth","Butterfield, Yaron S.N.","Carling, Tobias","Carlsen, Rebecca","Carter, Scott L.","Carty, Sally E.","Chan, Timothy A.","Chen, Amy Y.","Cherniack, Andrew D.","Cheung, Dorothy","Chin, Lynda","Cho, Juok","Chu, Andy","Chuah, Eric","Cibulskis, Kristian","Ciriello, Giovanni","Clarke, Amanda","Clayman, Gary L.","Cope, Leslie","Copland, John A.","Covington, Kyle","Danilova, Ludmila","Davidsen, Ta